public class ErpSyncFinalizer implements Finalizer {
    private Set<Id> accountIds;
    private Integer attempt;

    public ErpSyncFinalizer(Set<Id> accountIds, Integer attempt) {
        this.accountIds = accountIds;
        this.attempt = attempt;
    }

    public void execute(FinalizerContext context) {
        FB_Job_Log__c log = new FB_Job_Log__c(
            Job_Name__c = 'ErpAccountSyncJob',
            Attempt__c = attempt
        );

        if (context.getException() == null) {
            log.Status__c = 'Success';
        } else {
            log.Status__c = 'Failed';
            String errMsg = context.getException().getMessage();
            if (errMsg != null && errMsg.length() > 255) {
                errMsg = errMsg.left(255);
            }
            log.Error__c = errMsg;

            if (attempt < 3) {
                System.enqueueJob(new ErpAccountSyncJob(accountIds, attempt + 1));
            }
        }

        insert log;
    }
}
```

The root cause: in Apex, `new List<T>(n)` creates a list of **size n filled with nulls** (unlike Java where it's just capacity). So `new List<Account>(accountIds.size())` pre-fills with nulls, and then `.add()` appends after those nulls. The fix is to use `new List<Account>()`.

